title: The NOT gate
mode: vhdl
chapter: Signals and gates
check: behaves
---- intro
A **gate** is a part that looks at its inputs and decides its output. The simplest is the **NOT gate**: it turns a 1 into a 0, and a 0 into a 1. It always does the opposite.

| a | y |
|---|---|
| 0 | 1 |
| 1 | 0 |

In VHDL the gate is a word, written in front of the signal it turns round: `not a`.
---- task
Make `y` the opposite of `a`. Then click `a` on the right and watch the light do the opposite.
---- start
library ieee;
use ieee.std_logic_1164.all;

entity inverter is
  port(
    a : in  std_logic;
    y : out std_logic
  );
end inverter;

architecture rtl of inverter is
begin
    -- write it here
end rtl;
---- answer
library ieee;
use ieee.std_logic_1164.all;

entity inverter is
  port(
    a : in  std_logic;
    y : out std_logic
  );
end inverter;

architecture rtl of inverter is
begin
    y <= not a;
end rtl;
---- hint1
It is the line from the last lesson with one word added in front of `a`.
---- hint2
`y <= not a;`
---- solution
`y <= not a;` When `a` is 0 the light is on, and when `a` is 1 it is off.
---- done
The drawing on the right shows the NOT gate as a triangle with a small circle: the circle means "turned round". You will see that circle again.
